## The action grid your stores price on — this is critical

Your store managers do **not** price continuously. Each store picks a price from a **discrete grid of {{ grid.m }} levels**, from **{{ grid.lo }} €** to **{{ grid.hi }} €**, evenly spaced **{{ grid.step }} € apart**. Consequences you MUST respect:

- A change to `p_bar` **smaller than {{ grid.step }} €** snaps back to the same grid point and **moves no store** — it is wasted. To shift your stores up by one notch, raise `p_bar` by **at least {{ grid.step }} €**.
- Your price band must contain **at least 2 grid points** so your stores have room to react to local competition. A band narrower than {{ grid.step }} € on both sides collapses to a single forced price.
{% if grid.regime == 'CS' %}
- **Each chain type prices on its OWN grid.** Your grid runs {{ grid.lo }}–{{ grid.hi }} € (step {{ grid.step }} €). Rivals of other types sit on **shifted** grids: {% for ct, g in grid.other_chain_grids.items() %}{{ ct }} {{ g.lo }}–{{ g.hi }} € (step {{ g.step }} €){{ "; " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}. When you compare your price to a cross-type rival's, remember you are not on the same lattice.
{% else %}
- **All chains price on the same global grid** ({{ grid.lo }}–{{ grid.hi }} €, step {{ grid.step }} €), so your price and every rival's price are directly comparable, grid point for grid point.
{% endif %}

## Who your stores watch (and who they compete with)

Two different things, do not confuse them:

- **Competition for shoppers:** every one of your stores competes for demand against **all** nearby stores of every chain type — discount, standard, and bio alike — through travel distance and price. You never face "only" a subset.
- **What each store reacts to:** when a store sets its price, it conditions its decision on its **{{ graph.k }} most pivotal rival(s)** — {{ graph.match_explainer }}. {% if graph.mode == 'graph_states' %}On average your stores track **{{ graph.mean_observed }}** such rival(s) (max {{ graph.max_observed }}); **{{ graph.n_isolated }}** of your stores are local near-monopolists that watch no one and simply learn their own best price.{% else %}Each store reacts to its {{ graph.k }} nearest competitor(s).{% endif %}

This matters for coordination: a store can only *respond* to rivals it observes. If your stores observe same-type rivals ({{ graph.match }} = SC), holding an elevated same-type price is directly visible to the stores that can reciprocate. If they observe any-type rivals ({{ graph.match }} = A), a discounter cutting price is visible to your stores even across tiers.

